<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Guo Wei</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/904">904</a>–<a href="/years/954">954</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a> · Also known as Emperor Taizu of Later Zhou</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Guo Wei was a leading general of the Later Han. When the young emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liu-chengyou">Liu Chengyou</a> killed several senior ministers in 950 and plotted against him, slaughtering his family in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaifeng">Kaifeng</a>, Guo Wei marched on the capital, and early in 951 he founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/later-zhou">Later Zhou</a>, the last of the Five Dynasties. He eased harsh taxes and punishments, and was succeeded by his adopted son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chai-rong">Chai Rong</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/guo-wei.jpg" alt="Guo Wei" width="320" height="359" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Guo_Wei_(Zhou_Taizu).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Guo Wei was born on 10 September 904 in Yaoshan, in present-day Longyao County, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebei">Hebei</a>, as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tang-dynasty">Tang dynasty</a> broke apart into warring regions. His father, Guo Jian, prefect of Shunzhou under the warlord <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/li-keyong">Li Keyong</a>, was killed when the forces of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liu-rengong">Liu Rengong</a> took the city, and his mother, Lady Wang, died soon after; a distant relative, Lady Han, raised him. He grew into a strong young man more drawn to war than farming, fond of drinking, gambling and brawls, and was nicknamed Sparrow Guo for a sparrow-shaped tattoo on his neck.</p><p>At about 17, fleeing arrest, he joined the army of Li Jitao at Luzhou, who wanted brave soldiers more than he cared for the law and let him go free after he stabbed a butcher to death in a drunken quarrel.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/li-cunxu">Li Cunxu</a> founded the Later Tang in 923 and Li Jitao was killed, Guo Wei, then 19, was placed in the cavalry. Being literate and good at mathematics, he soon became an officer, and he read what he could on military strategy, guided by his sworn brother Li Qiong. In 927, when the emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-mingzong-of-later-tang">Li Siyuan</a> put down the rebellion of Zhu Shouyin, Guo was among the first to scale the wall of Xun under the general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shi-jingtang">Shi Jingtang</a>, who then put him in charge of military records. Under the Later Han of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liu-zhiyuan">Liu Zhiyuan</a> he served as Assistant Military Commissioner.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>After the teenage <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liu-chengyou">Liu Chengyou</a> came to the throne in 948, court intrigue let Guo Wei seize power in a coup, and he proclaimed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/later-zhou">Later Zhou</a> on New Year's Day of 951, 11 February in the modern calendar. Two of his sons, Guo Qingge and Guo Yige, had died on 24 December 950. He was the first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/han-chinese">Han Chinese</a> emperor in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> since 923. His rule was stable and well organised, and he passed reforms meant to ease the burdens on the peasantry. He died of illness on 22 February 954, three years into his reign.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Guo Wei” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Guo Wei</b>, a leading general of the Later Han, declared himself emperor and founded the Later Zhou, the last of the Five Dynasties.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/february-11">11 February in history</a></li><li><blockquote>In 950 Liu Chengyou had leading ministers killed and plotted against the general <b>Guo Wei</b>, who marched on Kaifeng.</blockquote><a href="/state/later-han">Later Han</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Guo Wei</b>, a leading general of the Later Han, was forced into rebellion when Emperor Liu Chengyou plotted to kill him in 950.</blockquote><a href="/state/later-zhou">Later Zhou</a></li><li><blockquote>When <b>Guo Wei</b> overthrew the Later Han in 951, Liu Chong, brother of its founder Liu Zhiyuan and governor of Hedong, proclaimed himself emperor at Taiyuan, refusing to recognise the Later Zhou and claiming to continue the Han dynasty.</blockquote><a href="/state/northern-han">Northern Han</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Longyao County, in what is now <a href="/countries/china">China</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaifeng">Kaifeng</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/china">China</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=37.35,114.76&amp;year=936" aria-label="Longyao County on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7421 819 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Guo Wei"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7871 1089h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7871 1089h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=37.35,114.76&amp;year=936">What was here in 936 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
